# Private-term blocklist — the leak gate's VOCABULARY layer.
#
# WHERE THIS FILE GOES, AND WHY IT IS A TEMPLATE RATHER THAN A REAL LIST.
# Copy it into your own individual practice set (the private repository
# named in your user-level config), fill it in there, and point the
# environment variable at it:
#
#     cp templates/leak-blocklist.txt.template \
#        <your individual set>/leak-blocklist.txt
#     export PRECEDENT_LEAK_BLOCKLIST=<your individual set>/leak-blocklist.txt
#     git config precedent.requireVocabulary true
#
# It must NOT be filled in here. A list of the words you are keeping private,
# committed to a public repository, publishes the words it exists to guard —
# and Precedent is a branch of a public repo, so a push cannot be taken back.
# The leak gate refuses a blocklist path inside this repository, by name.
# This is the same arrangement `scrub-gate` already uses: the blocklist lives
# in the private repo and scans the public tree from there.
#
# The `git config` line above matters as much as the export. Without it the
# vocabulary layer fails OPEN: a shell that starts without the variable set
# scans only the structural rules and exits 0, so the push goes through.
#
# FORMAT. One Python regular expression per line, matched case-insensitively
# against every file's text and every commit message in whatever the gate is
# scanning. Blank lines and lines beginning with `#` are ignored. An invalid
# expression fails the gate rather than being skipped.
#
# WHAT BELONGS IN IT. Words that are private *because of who they name*,
# which no structural rule can recognise: client and employer names, project
# code words, internal system and service names, unreleased product names,
# people's names where the association is the sensitive part, internal
# hostnames and ticket prefixes. The structural layer already covers shapes —
# email addresses, home directories, individual- and team-level paths — so
# there is no need to restate those here.
#
# WRITING THE PATTERNS. Prefer `\b…\b` so a term does not match inside an
# unrelated word. Cover the spellings the term actually appears in: hyphenated
# and spaced, singular and possessive, the abbreviation as well as the full
# name. A term that only ever appears one way in your notes today will appear
# another way the first time it matters.
#
# FOR A REPOSITORY NAME, WRITE THE STEM RATHER THAN THE WHOLE NAME, and end
# it `[\w-]*` rather than `\b`:
#
#     \bexamplename[\w-]*          not     \bExampleNameProject\b
#
# Upstream lost a name to exactly this on 2026-09-07. The full name was on
# the list; what reached a public branch was `<repo>-local`, the name a
# practice source had been given after the repository -- a SHORT form the
# full-name pattern does not begin to cover. (The trailing suffix was never
# the problem: `\bExampleNameProject\b` already matches
# `ExampleNameProject-local`, since a hyphen is a word boundary. The
# truncation is what does the work.) A private repository leaks through what
# is named AFTER it: a source, a branch, a directory, a tag, a check.
#
# CUT ONLY AS FAR AS THE EVIDENCE ALLOWS. Run the gate against the tree
# after each cut and read the hit count. A stem that is an ordinary English
# word matches innocent text everywhere, and a gate that fires on innocent
# text the day you install it is a gate you uninstall. Stop at the last cut
# that scores zero -- and reject even a zero-scoring cut that is short
# enough for a camelCase identifier to produce by accident. Record the
# counts beside the entry so the next person argues from evidence rather
# than re-deriving them.
#
# The gate helps: with an owner declared below, a run also NOTES any
# repository cloned on your disk, under that owner, whose bare name no
# pattern here matches. That note is the reminder to write this line.
#
# The entries below are placeholders, and are here to show the shape. Replace
# them with your own terms.
#
# They are deliberately spelled as unmistakable non-words rather than as
# plausible examples. A first draft of this file shipped `\bacme[- ]?corp\b`
# as its example, and the very first run of the gate failed — on "Acme Corp"
# in this repository's own style-guide template, which is placeholder text and
# not a leak. A gate that fails on innocent text the day you install it is a
# gate you uninstall, so the shipped examples cannot be words anything else
# might legitimately contain. If one of these ever fires, it means this file
# was copied and not edited.

\bexample-client-name-goes-here\b
\bexample-code-word-goes-here\b
\bEXAMPLE-TICKET-\d+\b

# --- REPOSITORY REFERENCES: an allowlist, and you must switch it on --------
#
# The patterns above are a BLOCKLIST: they block the terms you remembered to
# write down. That is the wrong default for repository names, and it failed
# in both directions in a single day (2026-09-07, upstream): a private
# repository nobody had listed was named in a public tree, and two names on a
# blocklist had since become public, forcing dozens of hits clearable only by
# deleting content about public files.
#
# So repository references are an ALLOWLIST instead. Uncomment the line below
# and put YOUR OWN GitHub account in it. Until you do, this half of the gate
# does nothing at all -- and it says so on every run rather than passing
# quietly, because a check that is off and silent is worse than one that is
# absent.
#
# visibility-audit: private-owner YOUR-GITHUB-ACCOUNT -- personal account; every repo under it is private unless allowed below
#
# With that line, every `YOUR-ACCOUNT/<name>` mention in a scanned tree is
# refused unless an allow line permits it, with a reason:
#
#   # visibility-audit: allow YOUR-ACCOUNT/precedent-team-NAME -- declared as a team source in precedent.json
#
# (Doubly commented so the parser skips it: a `# visibility-audit:` line
# that does not parse is now a hard failure, and the `<name>` placeholder
# this line used to carry does not parse -- angle brackets are not legal in
# a repository name. Left as a live directive it would fail the gate on a
# fresh copy of this template, which is the one moment nobody can debug it.)
#
# WHAT NOT TO PUT HERE, and it is the one that looks like it belongs.
# Do NOT pre-allow your INDIVIDUAL source. A shared repository is forbidden
# from declaring one -- tools/precedent_resolve.py refuses it by name, because
# naming it tells everyone with read access that it exists -- so an allow line
# for it hands out permission for the exact thing the architecture refuses.
# Add one only if a specific repository genuinely has to name it (upstream
# does, because it documents the model), and write the reason down.
#
# A REASON IS MANDATORY on every allow line. An accepted exposure nobody
# argued for is the same silence this whole file exists to replace.
